03125, 0.020721435546875, 0.0279083251953125, 0.04559326171875, -0.013397216796875, -0.01995849609375, 0.00434112548828125, -0.01120758056640625, -0.0018186569213867188, -0.0006966590881347656, 0.00717926025390625, 0.01116180419921875, 0.004985809326171875, -0.007080078125, -0.0021820068359375, 0.03338623046875, 0.0222015380859375, 0.01276397705078125, -0.00974273681640625, -0.00414276123046875, -0.0035076141357421875, 0.0116424560546875, -0.0135650634765625, 0.0064239501953125, -0.0016183853149414062, -0.0138397216796875, 0.021820068359375, 0.0017995834350585938, 0.0011119842529296875, -0.00014889240264892578, 0.04156494140625, 0.031463623046875, 0.00890350341796875, -0.022979736328125, -0.0043182373046875, 0.0196685791015625, -0.01190948486328125, 0.00725555419921875, -0.0037708282470703125, -0.00981903076171875, -0.006763458251953125, -0.01343536376953125, -0.01190948486328125, -0.01114654541015625, -0.017913818359375, 0.0179443359375, -0.055419921875, 0.017791748046875, -0.01114654541015625, -0.0197601318359375, 0.0089569091796875, -0.021514892578125, 0.00128173828125, -0.0274505615234375], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "\"\"\"\nMemory-based self-improving agents\u2014those that learn from an online stream of tasks and improve over time by maintaining a textual memory bank\u2014have shown great promise in recent literature. However, the reliability of these methods\u2014defined here as the consistency and predictability of agent performance across stochastic environments and task orderings\u2014has been critically overlooked. In this work, we address this gap by systematically evaluating the robustness (i.e., the degree to which agent performance is stable under varying conditions) of memory-based self-improving agents and proposing interventions to improve their reliability. We also introduce oversight mechanisms, defined as processes or tools that enable transparent monitoring and auditing of agent memory and behavior.\n\n**Summary of Key Terms and Operational Definitions**\n\nTo clarify terminology, we provide the following operational definitions, with concrete examples:\n\n| Term         | Definition                                                                 | Example                                                                                   |\n|--------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Reliability  | Consistency and predictability of agent performance across runs and task orderings | An agent consistently achieves similar task completion rates regardless of random seed or task sequence |\n| Robustness   | Stability of agent performance under varying conditions (e.g., random seeds, task order) | Performance variance (e.g., standard deviation) remains low when task order or environment changes |\n| Fragility    | High sensitivity of agent performance to stochasticity or task order, resulting in large performance variance | Agent\u2019s completion rate drops sharply when task order is shuffled or environment is perturbed |\n\n**Main Contributions**\n\nOur main contributions are as follows, each operationalized and evaluated as described:\n\n1. **Systematic Robustness Evaluation:**  \n   - We provide the first systematic evaluation of the robustness of memory-based self-improving agents to stochasticity and task order.  \n   - Robustness is quantified by measuring performance variance (standard deviation and confidence intervals) across multiple independent runs and randomized task orderings.  \n   - This evaluation reveals significant fragility\u2014defined as high sensitivity to stochastic factors and task sequence\u2014in current approaches.\n\n2. **Specification Interventions:**  \n   - We introduce and assess interventions involving enhanced task and environment specification.  \n   - Specifically, we integrate structured rubrics and explicit environment feedback into agent memory entries to mitigate observed fragility.  \n   - These interventions are implemented using automated templates and extraction algorithms, as detailed below, and their impact is empirically measured.\n\n3. **Evaluation Protocols:**  \n   - We propose and empirically validate standardized evaluation protocols, including reporting results across multiple runs and task orderings and using standardized benchmarks with randomized curricula.  \n   - These protocols are shown to improve the transparency and reproducibility of agent evaluation.\n\n4. **Oversight Mechanisms:**  \n   - We design and evaluate oversight mechanisms, such as human-auditable memory interfaces, that allow external reviewers to inspect, audit, and edit agent memory.  \n   - We measure their impact on the transparency of failure modes and the ease of diagnosing agent errors using both qualitative and quantitative metrics (see Methods and Results).\n\nCompared to prior studies, our work uniquely quantifies variance across runs and task orderings, analyzes the content and quality of agent memory using a reproducible rubric, and demonstrates the impact of specification interventions and oversight mechanisms on agent reliability.\n\n**Background and Methodological Details**\n\nWe focus on two representative memory-based methods:  \n(a) **Episodic Memory Augmented Agents (EMAA):** Agents store and retrieve textual summaries of past task experiences to inform future decisions. After each task, EMAA encodes a summary of the episode (e.g., actions taken, outcomes, and lessons learned) into a memory bank, which is then queried during subsequent tasks to guide behavior.  \n(b) **Self-Reflective Memory Agents (SRMA):** Agents maintain a memory bank of self-generated improvement notes, such as explicit reflections on failures or strategies for improvement, generated by prompting themselves to reflect on their performance after each task. The key difference is that EMAA focuses on factual episodic recall, while SRMA emphasizes metacognitive self-reflection.\n\n**Implementation Details and Modifications:**  \n- Both EMAA and SRMA are implemented following [Smith et al., 2022] and [Lee & Gupta, 2023], respectively.  \n- For this study, we extend both methods to support automated integration of structured rubrics and environment feedback into memory entries (see Specification Interventions).  \n- The memory retrieval module is adapted to handle the augmented memory format, and the memory bank size is fixed at 100 entries for all experiments.  \n- No other deviations from the original algorithms are made.\n\n**Methods**\n\nTo comprehensively re-evaluate these methods, we broaden the scope of evaluation along two axes:  \n1. Including multiple runs to quantify variance  \n2. Randomly shuffling the tasks to investigate the effect of task order\n\n**Environments:**  \n- We conduct experiments in two simulated environments\u2014GridWorld and TextWorld.  \n  - *GridWorld*: A discrete, partially observable navigation environment with stochastic transitions and obstacles, requiring agents to plan and adapt to uncertainty.  \n  - *TextWorld*: A text-based interactive fiction environment with complex, multi-step tasks involving object manipulation, exploration, and language understanding.  \n- Both environments are widely used benchmarks for evaluating generalization, memory, and reasoning in agents, and their stochasticity and task diversity closely mirror challenges faced in real-world continual learning scenarios.\n\n**Task Suite and Agent Architecture:**  \n- We use a suite of 20 multi-step tasks per environment, spanning a range of difficulty levels from simple navigation to complex multi-object manipulation.  \n  - *Justification*: 20 tasks provide a balance between diversity and tractability, consistent with standard practice in continual learning benchmarks [Smith et al., 2022; Lee & Gupta, 2023].  \n- For each method, we use a transformer-based agent architecture with 4 layers, 256 hidden units, and a memory retrieval module based on cosine similarity.  \n  - *Hyperparameters* (learning rate 1e-4, batch size 16, memory bank size 100) are held constant across all experiments to ensure comparability and are chosen based on prior work and preliminary tuning for stable training.  \n- We perform 10 independent runs per task ordering, and for each run, we randomly shuffle the task sequence to assess sensitivity to curriculum.  \n  - *Justification*: 10 runs are standard for estimating variance and statistical significance in reinforcement learning and continual learning studies.\n\n**Evaluation Metrics:**  \n- **Task Completion Rate:** The primary metric, defined as the percentage of tasks in which the agent achieves all specified success criteria (binary success per task; partial credit is not awarded).  \n- **Cumulative Reward:** The sum of environment-defined rewards accumulated across all tasks in a run.  \n- **Variance Metrics:** Standard deviation and 95% confidence intervals of task completion rate and cumulative reward across runs and task orderings.  \n- **Effect Size:** Cohen\u2019s d is used to quantify the magnitude of differences between conditions.  \n- **Transparency and Reproducibility:** For oversight mechanisms, we measure (a) the number of failure cases correctly identified by external reviewers, (b) inter-reviewer agreement (Cohen\u2019s kappa), and (c) the time required to diagnose errors using the memory interface.\n\n**Specification Interventions: Structured Rubrics and Environment Feedback**\n\nTo validate the hypothesis that underspecification contributes to fragility, we incorporate additional information into the memory construction process:\n\n- **Rubrics:** After each task, a structured rubric specifying task goals, constraints, and success criteria is generated automatically from the environment\u2019s metadata using a template-based extraction algorithm. The template is:  \n  ```\n  Goal: <task goal>. Constraint: <task constraints>. Success: <success criteria>.\n  ```\n  For example, \u201cGoal: Collect all coins. Constraint: Do not trigger alarms. Success: All coins collected, no alarms triggered.\u201d This rubric is appended to the agent\u2019s memory entry in a standardized format.\n\n- **Environment Feedback:** Explicit feedback, such as error messages and state changes after each action, is extracted from the environment logs using a rule-based parser. Each action-feedback pair is formatted as structured text (e.g., \u201cAction: Open door. Feedback: Door is locked.\u201d) and appended to the corresponding memory entry. Both the rubric and feedback integration processes are fully automated and require no manual intervention.\n\n**Manual Memory Analysis**\n\nTo analyze the agents' memory, we manually examine the memory bank contents after each run. Memory entries are coded using a rubric with three operationally defined criteria:\n- **Specificity:** The entry contains concrete, detailed information about the task (e.g., \u201cIn GridWorld, picking up the red key after opening the blue door leads to success\u201d vs. \u201cBe careful with keys\u201d).\n- **Relevance:** The entry is applicable to future tasks (e.g., \u201cAvoid moving north when a wall is present\u201d is relevant if similar obstacles recur).\n- **Correctness:** The entry accurately reflects the environment and outcomes (e.g., \u201cThe green button opens the exit\u201d is correct only if this is true in the environment).\n\nTwo annotators independently review the memory logs using this rubric, achieving a Cohen\u2019s kappa of 0.81 for inter-annotator agreement. Disagreements are resolved through a structured adjudication process: annotators first independently re-examine the disputed entries, then discuss their reasoning in a joint session. If consensus is not reached within 10 minutes, a third expert adjudicator is consulted. In our study, 12% of entries required adjudication, with an average resolution time of 6 minutes per disagreement.\n\n**Evaluation Protocols and Oversight Mechanisms**\n\n- **Standardized Evaluation Protocols:**  \n  - Results are reported across 10 independent runs and multiple randomized task orderings.  \n  - Standardized benchmarks with randomized curricula are used to ensure generalizability and reproducibility.\n\n- **Oversight Mechanisms:**  \n  - We implement a human-auditable memory interface that allows external reviewers to inspect, annotate, and edit agent"}}